    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes",
    "problem": "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$",
    "worked_solution": "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$",
